Keep the NVRAM parser out of the partitions the image does not have

The kernel stops in PE_init_iokit(), at the IONVRAM personality match,
before the EARLY_BOOT phase and long before bsd_init().  The last thing
the console shows is the NVRAM header dump that IONVRAM.cpp prints when
it has read the image:

    NVRAM : commonPartitionOffset - 0x..., commonPartitionSize - 0x800,
            systemPartitionOffset - 0xffffffff, systemPartitionSize - 0x0

The system partition is at 0xffffffff because the nio NVRAM image does
not carry one.  nio_adt.py builds that image with a common partition and
a free area, and no system partition, and XNU reads the partition list
and leaves the offsets of the partitions it did not find at 0xffffffff.

The size of the system partition was never initialized at all: the
constructor sets _commonPartitionSize and nothing else, so
_systemPartitionSize holds whatever was on the stack.  The loop that
walks the partitions therefore passes the size test with that garbage
value and reads _nvramImage + 0xffffffff, four gigabytes past an eight
kilobyte image.  The boot dies there without a word on the console.

Test the offset as well as the size, and initialize the two members the
constructor left alone, so a partition that is not there is skipped
instead of walked.  Both loops over the partitions need it: the one that
reads the variables and the one that writes them back.

--- a/iokit/Kernel/IONVRAMCHRPHandler.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IONVRAMCHRPHandler.cpp
@@ -320,7 +320,10 @@
 }
 
 IONVRAMCHRPHandler::IONVRAMCHRPHandler(OSSharedPtr<OSDictionary> &varDict) :
+	_commonPartitionOffset(0xFFFFFFFF),
 	_commonPartitionSize(0x800),
+	_systemPartitionOffset(0xFFFFFFFF),
+	_systemPartitionSize(0),
 	_varDict(varDict)
 {
 }
@@ -490,6 +493,7 @@
 	OSSharedPtr<const OSSymbol> propSymbol;
 	OSSharedPtr<OSObject>       propObject;
 	NVRAMRegionInfo             *currentRegion;
+	const uint8_t               *imageData;
 	NVRAMRegionInfo             variableRegions[] = { { kIONVRAMPartitionCommon, _commonPartitionOffset, _commonPartitionSize},
 							  { kIONVRAMPartitionSystem, _systemPartitionOffset, _systemPartitionSize} };
 
@@ -497,12 +501,26 @@
 
 	for (regionIndex = 0; regionIndex < ARRAY_SIZE(variableRegions); regionIndex++) {
 		currentRegion = &variableRegions[regionIndex];
-		const uint8_t * imageData = _nvramImage + currentRegion->offset;
 
-		if (currentRegion->size == 0) {
+		/*
+		 * nio: a partition that the image does not carry has no offset
+		 * and no size.  unserializeImage() leaves the offset at
+		 * 0xFFFFFFFF for a partition it did not find, and the size of a
+		 * partition it did not find was never initialized at all, so
+		 * the size test alone does not keep this loop out of the
+		 * partition.  Adding the offset to _nvramImage then reads
+		 * four gigabytes past the image, and the boot dies there with
+		 * no message.
+		 *
+		 * The nio NVRAM image carries a common partition and no
+		 * system one, so this is the branch that is always taken.
+		 */
+		if (currentRegion->size == 0 || currentRegion->offset == 0xFFFFFFFF) {
 			continue;
 		}
 
+		imageData = _nvramImage + currentRegion->offset;
+
 		DEBUG_INFO("region = %d\n", currentRegion->type);
 		cnt = 0;
 		while (cnt < currentRegion->size) {
@@ -627,7 +645,11 @@
 	for (regionIndex = 0; regionIndex < ARRAY_SIZE(variableRegions); regionIndex++) {
 		currentRegion = &variableRegions[regionIndex];
 
-		if (currentRegion->size == 0) {
+		/* nio: the same missing-partition case as in
+		 * unserializeVariables(): a partition the image does not
+		 * carry has no offset, and adding it to the image reads four
+		 * gigabytes past it. */
+		if (currentRegion->size == 0 || currentRegion->offset == 0xFFFFFFFF) {
 			continue;
 		}
 
